/* Original classroom drafts. Teacher review and full syllabus audit pending. */
const chapters = [
{id:'start',week:1,title:'Your first Python program',intro:'A program transforms inputs into useful outputs. Start with a small problem you can explain, then translate your steps into Python.',objectives:['Identify inputs, processing and outputs.','Run and edit a Python script.','Trace a sequence and compare expected with actual output.'],sections:[
['A problem before a program',`<p>A school club wants to estimate the cost of badges. Each badge costs $3. The program reads the number of badges as input, multiplies it by three and displays the cost as output. These steps form an algorithm: an ordered method for solving the problem.</p><p>Specify assumptions first: the number of badges is a non-negative whole number. Input validation will be added in week 7. A requirement describes what the solution must do; “display the cost for any valid badge quantity” is testable.</p>`],
['Prepare your workspace',`<p>Use your school's Python 3 environment, such as an approved editor or IDE. Create a course folder and save the example as <code>badge_cost.py</code>. Run the saved file using the editor's Run command, or <code>python badge_cost.py</code> in a terminal configured for Python. Type the quantity when the prompt appears.</p><p>Keep each task in its own file with a meaningful name. Save before running. Do not name a file after a standard Python module such as <code>random.py</code>.</p>`],
['Sequence: follow the order',`<p>Read the code from top to bottom. Assignment evaluates the right-hand side, then binds the result to the name on the left. <code>input</code> reads text; <code>int</code> converts suitable text into a whole number. We store the price as a named constant so its purpose is visible.</p><p>Predict the output for four badges before running the example. The result should be <code>Total: $12</code>. A program can run without crashing and still answer the wrong question: correctness means meeting the requirement.</p>`],
['Trace and test',`<table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Expected cost</th><th>Reason</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>$0</td><td>No badges</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>$3</td><td>One item</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>$12</td><td>Typical order</td></tr></table><p>Record the actual output alongside the expected output. If they differ, locate the first step where the values diverge. A syntax error prevents Python from understanding the instructions; a logic error can produce an incorrect answer even when execution succeeds.</p>`]
],code:'BADGE_PRICE = 3\nquantity = int(input("Number of badges: "))\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE\nprint(f"Total: ${total}")\n',practice:[['Predict','What happens when the quantity is 6?','The total is 18, so the output is Total: $18.'],['Modify','Add a fixed $2 packaging charge. Test quantities 0 and 3.','Use total = quantity * BADGE_PRICE + 2. Results are $2 and $11. Clarify whether packaging applies to a zero-item order; requirements determine that behaviour.'],['Explain','Why should you write an expected result before running the program?','An independent expected result lets you check correctness instead of accepting whatever the program prints.']],task:'Build a book-cover calculator: each cover costs $2 and every order has a $4 handling charge. State assumptions, write input/process/output steps, code the solution and record three tests.',solution:'COVER_PRICE = 2\nHANDLING = 4\nquantity = int(input("Covers: "))\ntotal = quantity * COVER_PRICE + HANDLING\nprint(f"Total: ${total}")\n# For quantities 0, 1, 5 expect 4, 6, 14.\n',recap:'Define the requirement, write the steps, implement a sequence, and compare actual results with independently calculated expectations.',lessons:['Diagnose prior knowledge and decompose the badge problem.','Set up the editor and run the example.','Trace, modify and test a sequential program.','Complete the book-cover task and explain the results.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html',refTitle:'Python built-in functions',quiz:{q:'Which step is processing in the badge program?',options:['Read the quantity','Multiply quantity by price','Display the total'],answer:1,explanation:'Processing transforms the input into the required result.'}},
{id:'values',week:2,title:'Values, types and operators',intro:'The same-looking input can mean different things. Choose a type that matches the meaning of the data and the operations you need.',objectives:['Choose integer, float, string and Boolean types.','Convert input and use arithmetic operators.','Explain assignment and operator precedence.'],sections:[
['Match the type to the meaning',`<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example</th><th>Use</th></tr><tr><td>int</td><td>24</td><td>Count of items</td></tr><tr><td>float</td><td>2.5</td><td>Measured quantity</td></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>"0042"</td><td>Identifier with leading zeros</td></tr><tr><td>bool</td><td>True</td><td>Two-state decision</td></tr></table><p>A numeric-looking identifier is often best kept as text because arithmetic has no useful meaning for it. Converting “0042” to an integer loses the leading zeros.</p>`],
['Convert deliberately',`<p><code>input()</code> produces a string. If a user enters 12, the result is the text <code>"12"</code>. Adding two strings joins them: <code>"12" + "3"</code> produces <code>"123"</code>. Convert valid numeric text before calculating.</p><p><code>int("12")</code> succeeds, while <code>int("12.5")</code> raises <code>ValueError</code>. <code>float("12.5")</code> succeeds. Validation and exception handling come later; do not assume all input is valid in a finished application.</p>`],
['Calculate and predict',`<p>Use <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code> and <code>/</code> for arithmetic. For the integer and float operands used here, division with <code>/</code> produces a float. Remainder <code>%</code> is useful when grouping items: 17 badges packed four per bag leave one badge after four full bags.</p><p>For this packing task, <code>//</code> gives the number of full bags. With non-negative integers it gives the whole-number quotient. In general Python floors this result, so negative cases require care. Multiplication and division bind more tightly than addition; parentheses make intent clear. <code>2 + 3 * 4</code> is 14; <code>(2 + 3) * 4</code> is 20.</p>`],
['Assignment updates a value',`<p><code>count = count + 1</code> reads the existing value, adds one, then updates the name. If count starts at 4, it ends at 5. This is assignment, not an equation to solve. <code>==</code> is the comparison operator.</p><p>Floating-point calculations approximate some decimal fractions. Formatting to two decimal places changes the displayed text, not the stored value. Our simple examples are for learning; financial applications need a deliberate representation such as integer cents or decimal arithmetic.</p>`]
],code:'badges = 17\nBAG_SIZE = 4\nfull_bags = badges // BAG_SIZE\nleft_over = badges % BAG_SIZE\nprint(f"Full bags: {full_bags}")\nprint(f"Remaining badges: {left_over}")\n',practice:[['Predict','What are 23 // 5 and 23 % 5?','4 and 3: four complete groups of five account for 20 items, leaving three.'],['Choose','Which type would you use for a postcode and why?','A string. It is an identifier; leading zeros may matter and arithmetic is not meaningful.'],['Debug','Why does input("Age: ") + 1 fail?','The input is a string and 1 is an integer. Convert valid input with int before arithmetic, and handle invalid input in a robust application.']],task:'A sports teacher has 29 cones and packs six per box. Print the number of full boxes and remaining cones. Then predict results for 0, 6 and 7 cones.',solution:'cones = 29\nBOX_SIZE = 6\nprint(cones // BOX_SIZE)\nprint(cones % BOX_SIZE)\n# 29 -> 4 and 5; 0 -> 0 and 0; 6 -> 1 and 0; 7 -> 1 and 1.\n',recap:'Data types determine available operations. Convert intentionally, distinguish assignment from comparison, and test arithmetic against hand-calculated results.',lessons:['Compare types and convert input.','Practise operators, precedence and trace updates.','Work on representation in the next chapter.','Complete conversion and representation practice.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html',refTitle:'Python standard types',quiz:{q:'What does "4" + "5" produce?',options:['9','"45"','A Boolean'],answer:1,explanation:'Both operands are strings, so + concatenates them.'}},
{id:'representation',week:2,title:'Numbers and characters in a computer',intro:'Binary, decimal and hexadecimal are different ways to write a number. Character encodings connect numbers with text.',objectives:['Convert small non-negative integers between bases.','Explain hexadecimal digits and place values.','Distinguish a character, a code point and encoded bytes.'],sections:[
['Place value changes with the base',`<p>In decimal, 247 means 2×100 + 4×10 + 7×1. Binary uses powers of two and only the digits 0 and 1. Binary 10110 means 1×16 + 0×8 + 1×4 + 1×2 + 0×1 = 22.</p><table><tr><th>Place</th><td>16</td><td>8</td><td>4</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><th>Digit</th><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr></table><p>Convert decimal 22 back by selecting 16, then 4, then 2. Each selected position receives 1; other positions receive 0. Leading zeros do not change a number's value: 00010110 is still 22.</p>`],
['Hexadecimal is a compact notation',`<p>Hexadecimal has sixteen digits: 0–9 followed by A–F for values 10–15. Hex 2F means 2×16 + 15 = 47. Each hexadecimal digit corresponds to four binary digits: 2 is 0010 and F is 1111, so 2F is 00101111.</p><p>Prefixes such as <code>0b</code> and <code>0x</code> identify binary and hexadecimal literals in Python. They are notation markers, not part of the number. A bit is a binary digit; a byte contains eight bits.</p>`],
['Text needs an agreed representation',`<p>A character is a symbol such as “A”. In Unicode, “A” has code point 65, written U+0041 in hexadecimal notation. Its decimal, binary and hexadecimal values are 65, 1000001 and 41. These are different representations of the same code-point number.</p><p>A code point is not automatically one byte. An encoding such as UTF-8 specifies how code points become bytes. ASCII-range characters such as A use one byte in UTF-8; many other characters require more. Do not assume every symbol or visible character occupies exactly eight bits.</p>`],
['Check by a second method',`<p>Show your place-value working before checking with Python. <code>bin</code> and <code>hex</code> format integers; <code>int(text, base)</code> interprets digits using a chosen base. <code>ord</code> obtains a single character's code point and <code>chr</code> reverses that mapping.</p><p>The example displays the value of A in three bases. A conversion check should preserve the original number, even though its written digits change.</p>`]
],code:'character = "A"\ncode_point = ord(character)\nprint(code_point)\nprint(bin(code_point))\nprint(hex(code_point))\nprint(chr(code_point))\n# Expected: 65, 0b1000001, 0x41, A\n',practice:[['Convert','Convert binary 11001 to decimal.','16 + 8 + 1 = 25.'],['Convert','Convert hexadecimal 3C to decimal and eight-bit binary.','3×16 + 12 = 60. Binary is 00111100.'],['Explain','Does Unicode code point 233 mean a UTF-8 file stores the character in one byte?','No. A code point identifies a character; UTF-8 defines its byte encoding. The character é, U+00E9, uses two UTF-8 bytes.']],task:'Convert decimal 37 into binary and hexadecimal by hand. Use Python to check both conversions and verify that converting back gives 37.',solution:'number = 37\nprint(bin(number))  # 0b100101\nprint(hex(number))  # 0x25\nprint(int("100101", 2))  # 37\nprint(int("25", 16))  # 37\n',recap:'Base changes the notation, not the value. Character encoding connects text to numeric representations, and byte storage depends on the encoding.',lessons:['Shared week 2: types and arithmetic first.','Use place values to convert numbers.','Group binary into four-bit hexadecimal digits.','Explain code points and check conversions.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/howto/unicode.html',refTitle:'Python Unicode HOWTO',quiz:{q:'What decimal value does hexadecimal 1A represent?',options:['10','26','110'],answer:1,explanation:'1×16 + 10 = 26.'}},
{id:'loops',week:4,title:'Repeating work with loops',intro:'Iteration lets a program repeat a useful task. Decide what repeats, what changes, and what makes the repetition stop.',objectives:['Choose a for or while loop for a task.','Use counters and accumulators.','Trace a loop and test its stopping condition.'],sections:[
['A fixed number of repetitions',`<p>A club records three donations. The same input-and-add operation repeats three times. Initialise the running total before the loop. Inside the loop, read the next amount and add it to the total. Display the result after the loop.</p><p><code>range(3)</code> supplies 0, 1 and 2. Its stop value is excluded. <code>range(1, 4)</code> supplies 1, 2 and 3. A counter records how many times something happens; an accumulator combines values, such as a sum.</p>`],
['Trace the running total',`<table><tr><th>Iteration</th><th>Donation</th><th>Total before</th><th>Total after</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>5</td><td>0</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>7</td><td>5</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>12</td><td>15</td></tr></table><p>If you set total to zero inside the loop, earlier amounts are lost. A trace table makes this mistake visible without guessing.</p>`],
['Repeat while a condition remains true',`<p>Use a <code>while</code> loop when the repetition count depends on a condition. For example, start with <code>count = 1</code>, repeat while <code>count &lt;= 3</code>, print count and increase it by one. The condition is checked before every pass, so the body may run zero times.</p><p>A loop may not terminate if nothing changes the state needed to make its condition false. Include an update, and trace the final successful test plus the first failed test. Test zero, one and several repetitions where the task allows them.</p>`],
['From algorithm to implementation',`<pre>total = 0\nFOR donation_number = 1 TO 3\n    INPUT donation\n    total = total + donation\nEND FOR\nOUTPUT total</pre><p>This original pseudocode follows the course guidance for assignment, keywords and block endings. FOR bounds are inclusive; Python range excludes its stop value. When explaining an algorithm, identify initialisation, repeated processing and termination.</p>`]
],code:'total = 0\nfor donation_number in range(1, 4):\n    donation = int(input(f"Donation {donation_number}: "))\n    total = total + donation\nprint(f"Total donations: ${total}")\n',practice:[['Predict','Which values appear in range(2, 6)?','2, 3, 4 and 5. The stop value 6 is excluded.'],['Debug','A total contains only the last donation. What should you inspect?','Check whether total = 0 occurs inside the loop. Initialise the accumulator once before repetition.'],['Trace','count starts at 1; while count < 3 prints count then increases it. What prints?','1 and 2. After the second update count is 3, so the condition becomes false.']],task:'Read four whole-number scores and display their total and mean. Before running, predict the result for 8, 10, 6 and 12. Assume valid scores.',solution:'total = 0\nfor score_number in range(4):\n    score = int(input("Score: "))\n    total += score\nmean = total / 4\nprint(total, mean)\n# Expected total 36, mean 9.0.\n',recap:'Initialise before repetition, update the changing state inside it, and test the point where repetition stops.',lessons:['Model fixed repetition with for and range.','Introduce condition-controlled while loops.','Trace counters/accumulators and repair mistakes.','Complete and test the score task.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html',refTitle:'Python control flow',quiz:{q:'Where should a running total normally be initialised?',options:['Inside every iteration','Once before the loop','After the result is printed'],answer:1,explanation:'Initialising once preserves accumulated values across iterations.'}},
{id:'functions',week:5,title:'Functions and scope',intro:'Give one useful task a name, define its inputs and return its result. Small functions make a program easier to understand and test.',objectives:['Define and call a function.','Distinguish parameters from arguments.','Use return values and explain local/global scope.'],sections:[
['One task, one function',`<p>Our club needs to calculate badge costs in more than one place. A function isolates this calculation. Its contract says: accept a valid quantity and unit price, return their product. It does not ask for input or print a receipt. Those tasks remain in the main program.</p><p>A parameter is a name in the definition. An argument is the value supplied by a call. In <code>badge_cost(4, 3)</code>, the arguments 4 and 3 are bound to parameters quantity and unit_price for that call.</p>`],
['Return a result',`<p><code>return</code> gives a value back to the caller and finishes that function call. The caller can store, display or combine the value. Printing a result is different: it displays text, but does not by itself return that result.</p><p>A function that finishes normally without returning an explicit value returns <code>None</code>. If your caller needs a number, design the function to return a number. A clear name such as <code>badge_cost</code> tells the reader the purpose.</p>`],
['Names belong to scopes',`<p>Parameters and names assigned inside this function are local. During the call, the quantity parameter is a local name, separate from a similarly named global. A global name is defined outside functions at module level. Functions can read global names, but unnecessary dependence on changing global state makes testing harder.</p><p>Prefer explicit parameters and return values. A local assignment does not automatically update a global name. Scope explains why a name may be unavailable in one part of the program even if it appeared elsewhere.</p>`],
['Test the part before the whole',`<p>Test the calculation separately: badge_cost(0, 3) should return 0; badge_cost(4, 3) should return 12. If it works, test the surrounding input and output. This narrows the source of failures.</p><p>Use comments to explain purpose or a non-obvious decision, rather than repeating the code. Indent the function body consistently. A short function that performs one logical task is easier to modify when requirements change.</p>`]
],code:'def badge_cost(quantity, unit_price):\n    return quantity * unit_price\n\nquantity = int(input("Number of badges: "))\ncost = badge_cost(quantity, 3)\nprint(f"Total: ${cost}")\n',practice:[['Identify','In def area(width, height) and area(5, 2), which are parameters and arguments?','width and height are parameters; 5 and 2 are arguments.'],['Explain','Why is return preferable to print for a reusable calculation?','The caller receives a result it can combine, test or display. Printing only produces output.'],['Predict','A function assigns x = 9 locally while global x is 2. Without a global declaration, what is global x after the call?','It remains 2. The local assignment binds a separate local name.']],task:'Write a delivery_charge function that returns 0 for an order total of at least 50 and 5 otherwise. Test 49.99, 50 and 50.01 without requesting input inside the function.',solution:'def delivery_charge(order_total):\n    if order_total >= 50:\n        return 0\n    return 5\n\nfor total in [49.99, 50, 50.01]:\n    print(total, delivery_charge(total))\n# Expected charges: 5, 0, 0.\n',recap:'Define a small contract, pass arguments into parameters, return results and keep local state local.',lessons:['Model function definitions and calls.','Trace parameters, arguments and returns.','Compare local and global names; refactor a program.','Complete boundary tests for delivery_charge.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#defining-functions',refTitle:'Python function definitions',quiz:{q:'What does a function with no explicit return normally return?',options:['0','None','The last printed value'],answer:1,explanation:'A normal completed call without an explicit return value returns None.'}},
{id:'arrays',week:6,title:'Working with one-dimensional arrays',intro:'Store related values together, then process them with a repeatable algorithm. We use Python lists to implement array-style tasks.',objectives:['Load, access and display values using indices.','Calculate a total with traversal.','Find the index of a minimum or maximum and state assumptions.'],sections:[
['An array idea, a Python implementation',`<p>A one-dimensional array-style structure gives each element one index. Python lists are our implementation tool. A Python list is dynamic and can contain mixed types; it is not identical to every language's fixed-size array. For these tasks, use a consistent element type and the syllabus's array conventions when writing pseudocode.</p><p>For scores = [12, 7, 19], index 0 holds 12, index 1 holds 7 and index 2 holds 19. The last valid index is len(scores) - 1. Accessing index 3 raises IndexError.</p>`],
['Load and traverse',`<p>Start with an empty list and append each accepted input. To display values, traverse the list. To include indices, iterate over range(len(scores)). Keep the distinction between a position and the value stored there: scores[1] is 7, not 1.</p><p>To total values, initialise an accumulator to zero, visit each element once and add it. A built-in sum is convenient, but you should also be able to explain and implement the standard traversal algorithm.</p>`],
['Remember the location of the best value',`<p>For a non-empty list, initialise best_index to 0. Compare each later value with the value at best_index; update the remembered index when a larger value appears. At the end, the remembered index locates the maximum.</p><p>Use the first value as the baseline rather than zero: a list containing only negative values still needs a correct maximum. With a strict greater-than comparison, ties keep the first maximum. Replacing it with greater-than-or-equal would keep the last. State the chosen tie rule.</p>`],
['Check edge cases',`<p>For [12, 7, 19, 19], the total is 57 and the first maximum is at index 2. Test a one-element list, equal values and negative values. An empty list has a valid total of zero but no maximum position; handle it before reading index 0.</p><p>An index is zero-based in our Python examples. If a user expects “item number 3”, add one when displaying index 2. Do not change the indexing inside the algorithm to match a display label.</p>`]
],code:'scores = [12, 7, 19, 19]\ntotal = 0\nfor score in scores:\n    total += score\nprint("Total:", total)\n\nif len(scores) == 0:\n    print("No maximum: the list is empty")\nelse:\n    best_index = 0\n    for index in range(1, len(scores)):\n        if scores[index] > scores[best_index]:\n            best_index = index\n    print("First maximum index:", best_index)\n    print("Maximum value:", scores[best_index])\n',practice:[['Predict','For [5, 9, 9], which index does the strict comparison algorithm return?','1, the first maximum. An equal value does not update the index.'],['Debug','Why can a maximum algorithm initialised to value 0 fail for [-8, -3]?','Zero is larger than every actual element. Start from a real element after checking the list is non-empty.'],['Trace','What is the final total for [4, -2, 6]?','8: accumulator states are 0, 4, 2 and 8.']],task:'Load four integer temperatures into a list, print each index/value, and find the first minimum index without using min. Test [-2, 4, -5, -5].',solution:'temperatures = []\nfor index in range(4):\n    temperatures.append(int(input("Temperature: ")))\nfor index in range(len(temperatures)):\n    print(index, temperatures[index])\nlowest_index = 0\nfor index in range(1, len(temperatures)):\n    if temperatures[index] < temperatures[lowest_index]:\n        lowest_index = index\nprint(lowest_index, temperatures[lowest_index])\n# Expected first minimum: index 2, value -5.\n',recap:'Traverse systematically, distinguish indices from values, and specify empty-input and tie behaviour.',lessons:['Load and display an array-style list.','Trace and implement an accumulator.','Locate minimum/maximum positions.','Test edge cases and complete the temperature task.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html',refTitle:'Python lists and data structures',quiz:{q:'For values = [8, 3, 6], what is values[1]?',options:['8','3','1'],answer:1,explanation:'Index 1 is the second element, whose value is 3.'}},
{id:'files',week:7,title:'Text files and reliable input',intro:'A program should preserve useful results and respond clearly when input is unsuitable. Combine file processing with deliberate validation.',objectives:['Read, write and append sequential text files.','Distinguish type checking from range checking.','Handle expected conversion errors and test invalid input.'],sections:[
['Accept only usable input',`<p>A quantity can have the right type but the wrong range. The text “-3” converts to an integer, but a negative badge quantity violates our requirement. The text “three” fails conversion. Handle these separately: attempt int conversion, then test quantity &gt;= 0.</p><p>Catch the expected ValueError rather than every possible error. A broad catch can conceal mistakes in the program. A helpful message explains what the user should enter. Validation reduces unsuitable input; it cannot guarantee that a plausible quantity is truthful.</p>`],
['A file outlives the program',`<p>A variable in memory is temporary. A saved file can be opened by a later run. Our text file stores one quantity per line. This simple format requires agreement: every line should contain one valid non-negative integer.</p><p>Paths identify file locations. A relative filename such as quantities.txt is resolved against the program's current working directory, which may differ from the script's folder. Use a dedicated practice folder so writing an example cannot replace an important file.</p>`],
['Choose the mode deliberately',`<table><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Important behaviour</th></tr><tr><td>r</td><td>Read</td><td>Missing file causes an error</td></tr><tr><td>w</td><td>Write</td><td>Existing contents are replaced</td></tr><tr><td>a</td><td>Append</td><td>New text goes at the end</td></tr></table><p>The with statement closes the file when its block finishes, including when an error occurs. Explicit UTF-8 encoding makes the intended text encoding clear. A newline separates records; forgetting it can join two quantities into one.</p>`],
['Read records in order',`<p>To process a sequential file, open it for reading and visit lines in order. For each line, strip surrounding whitespace, convert its text and apply the calculation. If a file may contain malformed data, decide whether to reject the file, report a line number or skip an invalid record. Do not silently discard data without a stated policy.</p><p>Test valid input, zero, a negative value, letters and a decimal string. For files, test one record, several records and an empty file. Predict what a second run will do in append mode.</p>`]
],code:'while True:\n    try:\n        quantity = int(input("Non-negative quantity: "))\n    except ValueError:\n        print("Enter a whole number.")\n        continue\n    if quantity < 0:\n        print("Quantity cannot be negative.")\n        continue\n    break\n\nwith open("quantities.txt", "a", encoding="utf-8") as file:\n    file.write(f"{quantity}\\n")\nprint("Quantity saved.")\n',practice:[['Explain','Why does int("-4") not provide sufficient validation?','It succeeds as a type conversion, but -4 is outside the required non-negative range.'],['Predict','What happens if you use w instead of a on a second run?','The old contents are replaced when the file is opened, rather than retained and extended.'],['Test','Name a useful input that checks conversion failure.','Examples include three or 2.5. Neither is valid integer text for int conversion.']],task:'Create a practice file with three lines: 2, 0 and 5. Read it sequentially and display the total. State the file-format assumptions and then test an empty file.',solution:'total = 0\n# Assumption: every line contains a valid non-negative integer.\nwith open("quantities.txt", "r", encoding="utf-8") as file:\n    for line in file:\n        total += int(line.strip())\nprint("Total:", total)\n# Three supplied records -> 7. Empty file -> 0.\n# A missing file raises FileNotFoundError; malformed lines need a policy.\n',recap:'Validate type and range separately. Choose a file mode intentionally, close files reliably and document the record format.',lessons:['Model read/write/append and file paths.','Add range validation and specific exception handling.','Combine file processing with a test table.','Controlled practical: adapt a fresh problem using weeks 1–7 skills.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/inputoutput.html#reading-and-writing-files',refTitle:'Python file input and output',quiz:{q:'Which mode retains existing text and adds new records at the end?',options:['r','w','a'],answer:2,explanation:'Append mode adds text at the end without replacing the existing contents.'}}
];
